{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790888571,"nonce":"W3uTJyWAMBxynFr/pWsBSFoWVzw=","title":"vmalert spells keep_firing_for in snake_case and oto reads only Prometheus's keepFiringFor, so every vmalert rule snapshot records keep_firing_for as 0 and fingerprints differently from the same rule on Prometheus","message":"**What is wrong.** Prometheus's rules API tags the field `keepFiringFor` (`web/api/v1/api.go`, `json:\"keepFiringFor\"`); vmalert's `ApiRule` tags it `json:\"keep_firing_for\"` (`app/vmalert/rule/web.go`). oto's `wireRule` (`internal/sources/client/prometheus/client.go:145`) reads only `keepFiringFor`, so the value decodes as 0.\n\n**The demonstration.** A vmalert rule with `keep_firing_for: 5m` produces a RuleSnapshot with `keep_firing_for_seconds = 0`; `ComputeRuleFingerprint` (`internal/alerts/domain/keys.go:343`) folds that 0 into the fingerprint.\n\n**Why it matters.** A RuleSnapshot is \"what the rule said at that moment\" (ADR 0009); this one says something false, silently.\n\n**Where.** `client.go` wire decoding (accept both spellings, prefer the one present); a vmalert-shaped fixture in the client tests.\n\n**Scope.** small.\n\n**Done when.** A vmalert fixture decodes `keep_firing_for: 300` as 300; a Prometheus fixture is unchanged; fingerprint goldens for Prometheus unchanged.\n\n**Blocked by.** None — can start immediately.\n\n**Mode.** AFK — can be implemented and merged without a human decision. Source: ADR 0052/0053/0054 design session, 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}